      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The observable macroscopic sign of evaporation is the loss or disappearance of visible liquid water from a surface, because the resulting vapor is invisible.",
      "evidence": "When molecules escape to the gas phase the liquid volume diminishes; the gas phase cannot be seen directly.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Among the four statements, only option (D) – “Water droplets disappear from the grass” – directly describes the loss of visible liquid water from a surface.",
      "evidence": "Option A describes rain falling (precipitation), B describes a clear sky (weather condition), C describes droplets appearing (condensation); none convey disappearance of liquid.",
      "weight": 0.35,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "In the context of a simple temperature‑evaporation investigation, the disappearance of droplets on grass is interpreted as evaporation rather than absorption or wind removal.",
      "evidence": "Educational questions about evaporation focus on the phase‑change explanation; absorption is not a phase change and is not implied by the wording.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": [3]
    }
  ],
  "answer": "D",
